als and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"accesses on ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"n"}]},{"text":" elements costs O((","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"m"}]},{"text":" + ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"n","type":"text"}]},{"text":") log ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"n"}]},{"text":") in all, so each takes O(log ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"n","type":"text"}]},{"text":")","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"amortized, and recently or frequently accessed elements stay near the root: accessing the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"elements in ascending order costs O(","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"n"}]},{"text":") in all. Reads (","type":"text"},{"code":"contains(_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", the bounds, the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"subscripts and iteration) leave the tree as it is, so they keep the value semantics and the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"API of the other sorted sets, and take time proportional to the element’s depth, which a run","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"of ascending insertions can make ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"n","type":"text"}]},{"text":". Use ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/BinarySearchTrees\/documentation\/BinarySearchTrees\/SplayTree\/access(_:)","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"text":" to look an element up and splay it.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"code":["var recent: SplayTree = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]","recent.access(40)             \/\/ 40, now at the root","recent.contains(25)           \/\/ false, and the tree is unchanged","let parts = recent.split(at: 30)","Array(parts.lower)            \/\/ [10, 20]","Array(parts.upper)            \/\/ [30, 40, 50]"],"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The elements are a random-access collection in ascending order, indexed by rank like an","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"array: ","type":"text"},{"code":"tree[0]","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is the smallest element. The vertices live in flat arrays with ","type":"text"},{"code":"Int32","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"links, so the tree holds fewer than 2³¹ − 1 elements, and copying it copies a few arrays only","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"when one copy is mutated.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}],"kind":"content"}],"abstract":[{"text":"A sorted set kept in a splay tree: a binary search tree that rotates each element it","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"accesses, inserts or removes up to the root (Sleator and Tarjan, 1985).","type":"text"}],"references":{"doc://BinarySearchTrees/SQ":{"type":"unresolvable","identifier":"doc:\/\/BinarySearchTrees\/SQ","title":"Swift.Equatable"},"doc://BinarySearchTrees/documentation/BinarySearchTrees/RedBlackTree":{"role":"symbol","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A sorted set kept in a red–black tree: a binary search tree whose vertices are red or black,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"with no red vertex under a red one and the same number of black vertices on every path from the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"root down (Guibas and Sedgewick, 1978)."}],"fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"RedBlackTree","kind":"identifier"}],"title":"RedBlackTree","type":"topic","kind":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"RedBlackTree","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentation\/binarysearchtrees\/redblacktree","identifier":"doc:\/\/BinarySearchTrees\/documentation\/BinarySearchTrees\/RedBlackTree"},"doc://BinarySearchTrees/documentation/BinarySearchTrees/Treap":{"role":"symbol"